export const priorityArticles = {
  "joyagoo-shoes-spreadsheet-size-qc-guide": {
    title: "Joyagoo Shoes Spreadsheet 2026: Size, Option and QC Checks",
    description: "Use a Joyagoo shoes spreadsheet to shortlist footwear, verify the live option, compare sizing evidence and prepare a focused warehouse QC review.",
    updated: "September 21, 2026",
    read: "12 min read",
    sections: [
      ["Use the spreadsheet to shortlist, not to approve", "A Joyagoo shoes spreadsheet is useful because it turns a broad search into a manageable set of product routes. It does not prove that a pair is in stock, that the pictured variation is selectable, that the seller will send the same batch, or that the shoe will fit. Start by saving no more than three candidates in the same footwear category. Record the card title, destination, displayed option and the date you checked it. Then reopen every live listing. A route that still loads but now shows a different model is not a valid match. Compare the main image, color blocking, sole shape, fastening system and option names before keeping it. This shortlisting step prevents a common mistake: treating a familiar thumbnail as confirmation. The spreadsheet should reduce browsing time while the current listing, selected variation and later warehouse evidence carry the decision."],
      ["Record the exact shoe option before payment", "Footwear listings often combine several colors, materials, sizes and packaging choices on one page. The lowest visible price may belong to a different variation from the preview that attracted you. Write a one-line order record containing model, color, seller size label, quantity and whether the original box is included. Preserve the seller's wording when two options look similar; do not replace an unclear version name with your own guess. Recheck the payable item price and domestic shipping in the live order flow because a spreadsheet amount is only a reference. If the desired option has disappeared, pause instead of choosing the nearest alternative. A clear record gives you something concrete to compare with the order confirmation and QC photos. It also makes support communication more efficient because the issue can be described as a specific mismatch rather than a general complaint."],
      ["Compare measurements instead of trusting one size label", "A printed EU, UK, US or CN number is not a universal measurement. Seller conversions can differ, and two shoes with the same label may have different internal length or toe shape. Use a well-fitting pair as the baseline. Measure its removable insole from heel to the longest point, note the width at the broadest area, and record whether the fit is close, regular or roomy. Compare those figures with the current seller chart and identify which unit the chart uses. Foot length and insole length are not interchangeable, so label both clearly. Leave practical room for socks and the intended fit, but do not invent a fixed conversion rule. If the listing lacks usable measurements, treat sizing as unresolved. A targeted warehouse measurement can improve the evidence, although it still cannot reproduce the feel of wearing the shoe."],
      ["Build a category-specific QC list", "Warehouse photos become more useful when the checks match the shoe. For sneakers, inspect the toe shape, left-right symmetry, eyelets, tongue labels, heel alignment, midsole joins and outsole pattern. For loafers or dress shoes, add welt or edge finishing, vamp symmetry and hardware. For boots, inspect shaft height, zips, hooks and both sides of the upper. Always confirm quantity, color, size tag and the presence of included parts before looking at small cosmetic details. Review the pair together and each shoe separately. Camera angle can make one side appear longer, so look for repeated evidence across multiple views. If a decisive area is hidden, request one purposeful image or measurement through the current account tools if available. QC can show visible condition and order accuracy; it cannot guarantee comfort, authenticity, long-term durability or consistency with a future batch."],
      ["Decide whether the shoe box is worth its volume", "The original box may protect structure and keep accessories together, but it can increase packed dimensions. That matters because some international lines compare actual weight with a dimension-based billing weight. Do not remove a box automatically. Consider the shoe's structure, resale or gift purpose, decorative packaging, route limits and the protection available after removal. Soft slides may tolerate compact packing better than rigid sneakers, tall boots or shoes with delicate hardware. If the box is not essential, ask what replacement protection will prevent crushing and abrasion. If it is essential, include its likely volume in the parcel plan. The correct choice is a risk decision, not a universal money-saving trick. Keep the decision beside the QC record so the warehouse instruction does not contradict what you approved."],
      ["Review the return action while evidence is current", "Joyagoo's published shopping guidance says buyers should review warehouse QC promptly, and its return page currently describes a five-day, 120-hour request window after the status changes to QC Completed for eligible items. Eligibility, seller agreement, packaging condition and the action visible in the order still matter. Open the current order instead of relying on an article timer. If the pair is wrong, identify the precise evidence: a different size tag, missing shoe, incorrect color, visible crack or option mismatch. Save the relevant photo numbers and submit the available action while it is present. Do not remove tags or packaging before the decision is final. A vague statement such as “not good” is weaker than a dated comparison between the order record and the received pair. Current account controls and official policy always take priority over this independent checklist."],
      ["Connect the pair to the parcel decision", "Once the shoes are accepted and stored, keep the order record, QC notes and packaging choice together. Check which routes remain available for the destination and product mix. Compare billing method, restrictions, tracking, insurance terms, size limits and protection rather than choosing only from the first displayed price. A large box can change the parcel's dimensions even when the shoes are light, while reinforcement can add both weight and volume. If several pairs are consolidated, consider how boxes stack and whether a heavy item can deform a softer one. The estimate shown before final packing is not a guaranteed total; the final packed measurements and logistics provider rules control the billing basis. Saving the estimated and final figures makes the next shoe shortlist more accurate."],
      ["Keep a repeatable shoe decision record", "For every shortlisted pair, keep eight fields: spreadsheet route, live destination, check date, exact option, size evidence, QC result, return deadline shown in the account and packaging choice. Mark unresolved fields instead of filling them with assumptions. This record makes stale links obvious and keeps size advice separate from warehouse proof. It also supports a clean sequence: discover in the spreadsheet, verify the listing, submit the exact option, inspect the received pair, act within the available after-sales window and prepare suitable packing. That sequence is more valuable than collecting hundreds of unreviewed shoe links. A smaller list with current destinations and explicit checks gives buyers a faster route to a defensible decision."],
      ["Avoid the three shortcuts that cause most shoe mistakes", "The first shortcut is choosing a familiar size number without checking the seller's chart or measurement system. The second is approving from one attractive warehouse angle while the size tag, opposite side or outsole remains unclear. The third is removing the box before deciding whether the pair needs structural protection or could still require an eligible return. Replace each shortcut with a dated checkpoint. Before payment, compare a personal shoe measurement with the seller evidence and save the exact option. At QC, inspect identity and both shoes before small cosmetic details. Before parcel submission, confirm that the after-sales decision is closed and write a specific packing instruction. If any checkpoint remains unresolved, pause at that stage instead of allowing the next status to make the decision harder. This disciplined sequence does not make sizing or quality certain, but it reduces preventable mismatches and preserves a clear record for support. It also keeps the spreadsheet honest: a row is a route to current evidence, not a badge that the product has already passed every later check."],
    ],
    sources: [
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Guidance", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/shopping-assistant-guidance/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Return Policy", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/terms-of-promised-returns-with-no-reasons/" },
      { title: "Actual and Volumetric Weight", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/actual-weight-and-volumetric-weight/" },
    ] satisfies Source[],
  },
  "joyagoo-clothing-spreadsheet-size-guide": {
    title: "Joyagoo Clothing Spreadsheet 2026: Hoodies, T-Shirts and Size Checks",
    description: "Turn Joyagoo clothing spreadsheet finds into a measured shortlist with live-option, fabric, print, QC and parcel checks for hoodies and T-shirts.",
    updated: "September 21, 2026",
    read: "12 min read",
    sections: [
      ["Start with one clothing category and one intended fit", "A Joyagoo clothing spreadsheet can surface many hoodies and T-shirts quickly, but a long list is not yet a useful comparison. Begin with the garment type and the fit you actually want: cropped, regular, oversized, boxy or long. Save three candidates that appear to serve the same purpose. Reopen their current listings and confirm the image, product title and available variations still correspond to the spreadsheet entry. Remove a route if it redirects, has lost the required color or now shows another garment. This keeps the shortlist comparable and stops an old thumbnail from doing the work of a live listing. Record the check date because seller pages and option structures change. The spreadsheet is the discovery layer; the current product page supplies the option evidence needed before an order."],
      ["Translate labels into measurements", "Size letters are weak comparison tools across different sellers. Build a table with shoulder width, chest width, garment length and sleeve length for tops. Add waist, rise and inseam when the item is trousers or shorts. Note whether the seller reports a flat measurement or a full circumference, and keep centimeters and inches clearly separated. Measure a garment that already fits the intended way, using the same points, rather than comparing the chart directly with body measurements. Fabric stretch, lining and construction can still change the feel, but this method creates a stronger baseline than choosing the usual letter. If the chart is an image, preserve the original labels beside your notes so a translation error does not silently change the measurement. Where no reliable chart exists, classify fit as unresolved rather than inventing a conversion."],
      ["Confirm color, version and graphic placement", "Clothing pages may place several colors, washes, prints or seasonal versions under one title. Match the variation thumbnail and seller option text with the product you intend to submit. Check whether front and back graphics are part of the same option, whether a hoodie is pullover or zip-up, and whether a set price includes one piece or several. For printed garments, record the expected graphic location and broad proportions without treating seller images as exact production proof. For embroidered items, note the expected area, orientation and color. Lighting can shift dark colors, so use multiple listing images and later multiple warehouse photos. A good order record contains product, color, size, version and quantity in separate fields. That structure makes a wrong option easy to identify when the item reaches the warehouse."],
      ["Read fabric claims cautiously", "Seller descriptions can include fiber percentages, weight, lining, wash effects or seasonal labels, but a listing statement is not the same as a laboratory result. Preserve the seller's current description and avoid upgrading terms such as “cotton blend” into “100% cotton.” Look for internal label photos when available and note whether the garment is brushed, loopback, lined or unlined. These details affect warmth, drape and packed volume. Color and texture can also vary between studio images and warehouse lighting. Use descriptions to frame questions, not to guarantee performance. Warehouse QC can confirm the visible label, broad texture and construction but normally cannot prove long-term shrinkage, pilling or exact fabric composition. If a material detail is essential, treat missing evidence as a reason to pause or request clarification rather than filling the gap with a community claim."],
      ["Review clothing QC in a fixed order", "Begin with identity: garment type, selected color, size label, version and quantity. Then inspect the front, back, sides, collar or hood, cuffs, hem, pockets, zip and internal labels. Check print or embroidery placement, obvious stains, holes, open seams and missing parts. For a hoodie, compare drawstrings, hood shape and pocket alignment; for a T-shirt, focus on collar shape, sleeve symmetry and graphic position. A flat photograph can distort length or hide thickness, so use measurement evidence where fit matters. Ask for a targeted image only when it can change the decision. QC photos show the received unit at one moment. They do not guarantee fit, comfort, fabric chemistry, durability or the appearance of another order. Keeping those limits visible reduces both false confidence and unnecessary rejection over camera-angle effects."],
      ["Act before the available return window closes", "Joyagoo's current help material states that eligible return or exchange requests should be made within five days after QC completion, with the return page describing the same period as 120 hours. The live order action, seller rules and product condition determine whether the request can proceed. Check photos soon after they appear. When reporting a problem, identify the exact mismatch and image: wrong size tag, missing back print, damaged zip, visible stain or different color. Preserve tags and packaging while the decision is open because some categories require resale condition. Charges and seller responsibility can vary, so review the current policy and account balance instead of quoting an old flat amount. The aim is not to return every imperfect garment; it is to make a timely evidence-based choice."],
      ["Plan packing from the garment mix", "Clothing is often compressible, but the full parcel can still become bulky when it includes thick fleece, padded jackets, shoe boxes or rigid accessories. Before selecting a route, separate items that can be folded or vacuum packed from items whose construction could be damaged by compression. Ask whether the original packaging adds useful moisture or abrasion protection. Check route eligibility and current value-added services in the live flow. International shipping is calculated later than the product purchase and can depend on destination, packed weight, volume and route rules. A low product price does not predict a low landed cost. Save the warehouse weight, requested packaging and final parcel measurements so you can see which garment types created volume. That record improves later spreadsheet choices without pretending one haul represents every route."],
      ["Use a compact clothing worksheet", "A practical worksheet has one row per item and columns for live URL, check date, garment type, desired fit, exact color, seller size, flat measurements, fabric claim, graphic details, QC result and packing choice. Add a note when a field is unknown. Do not copy a size recommendation from another listing into the row. This format makes near-duplicate hoodies or T-shirts easier to compare and reveals when one candidate lacks the measurements needed for a sensible decision. It also connects discovery with action: shortlist from the spreadsheet, verify the live option, compare measurements, inspect warehouse evidence, use the available after-sales process and then plan the parcel. The value comes from maintaining those connections, not from maximizing the number of saved links."],
      ["Run a final garment comparison before ordering", "Place the shortlisted rows side by side and eliminate any candidate that cannot answer the essential questions. Does the live page still match the spreadsheet card? Is the desired color and version selectable? Are the measurement points understandable and comparable with a garment you own? Does the listing explain whether the graphic, lining or included pieces belong to that option? If two candidates remain, prefer the one with clearer current evidence rather than the one with the most persuasive studio image. Save the rejected reason so the same stale or ambiguous route does not return to the shortlist later. After ordering, do not overwrite the pre-purchase record; add the order and QC evidence as new stages. That history helps distinguish a seller-page change from a warehouse mismatch. It also gives the article workflow a measurable outcome: fewer accidental variations, quicker QC review and packaging choices based on the actual garment mix. No spreadsheet can guarantee fit, but a disciplined comparison can make the uncertainty visible before payment instead of discovering it only after international delivery."],
    ],
    sources: [
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Guidance", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/shopping-assistant-guidance/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Return Policy", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/terms-of-promised-returns-with-no-reasons/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Value Added Services", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/value-added-services/" },
    ] satisfies Source[],
  },
  "joyagoo-warehouse-storage-guide": {
    title: "Joyagoo Warehouse Storage Guide: Plan Around the 90-Day Window",
    description: "Track Joyagoo warehouse storage from the Stored status, coordinate arrivals, consolidation and parcel payment, and avoid relying on the newest item's clock.",
    updated: "September 21, 2026",
    read: "12 min read",
    sections: [
      ["Start the clock from the correct status", "Joyagoo's current warehouse policy says an item receives 90 days of free storage beginning when its order status changes to Stored. That is more precise than counting from payment, seller dispatch or Stock Arrived. Save the stored date for every item as soon as it appears and calculate a review date well before the published deadline. Do not use the date of the newest arrival for the entire group. A parcel with six items has six item clocks until those items are submitted together. The earliest stored item normally creates the planning constraint. Policies and account displays can change, so confirm the live date and current extension option instead of relying only on a calendar made from this article. A simple dated inventory prevents a long consolidation plan from turning into an urgent packing decision."],
      ["Separate item storage from unpaid parcel storage", "The same official page distinguishes stored items from a parcel that has already been submitted but has not had its shipping fee paid. It currently states that such a submitted parcel has a 30-day free storage period before payment. These are different stages and should occupy different rows in your tracker. Before submission, you can still decide which stored items belong together. After submission, the warehouse has a parcel instruction and payment becomes the next responsibility. Do not assume that submitting a parcel resets every deadline to another 90 days. Record the parcel submission date, payment status and the countdown shown in the account. If the packing result or route requires attention, contact support while the record is current rather than allowing an unpaid parcel to sit without communication."],
      ["Build an earliest-deadline dashboard", "Use one row per order with product name, order number, Stored date, free-storage end date shown or calculated, QC decision, restriction note and intended parcel group. Sort by the earliest deadline. Add a weekly review flag when any item is within thirty days of its planning date, not only when it is near expiry. This creates room for a late seller, a return decision, route research and packing questions. Mark products still at Purchased, Seller Sent, Stock Arrived or QC Completed separately because their storage clock has not yet reached the same stage. The official shopping flow uses those statuses for different events. A dashboard should reflect that sequence instead of merging every paid item into “warehouse.” Clear stage labels make it obvious whether the next action belongs to the seller, warehouse, buyer or parcel process."],
      ["Consolidate compatible items, not every item", "Storage makes consolidation possible, but the largest possible parcel is not automatically the best parcel. Group items that qualify for compatible routes, need compatible protection and can tolerate the same packing method. A fragile accessory may need separation from heavy footwear. A restricted product may remove otherwise suitable routes from the whole parcel. A large shoe box can dominate volume even when the rest is compressible clothing. Before grouping, record each item's stored weight, dimensions if available, restriction status, box decision and declared description. Compare a combined scenario with a split scenario using the live route options. Avoid universal cost claims because destination, route and packing details change the result. The goal is a parcel whose eligibility, protection and deadline can all be explained."],
      ["Use extensions as a planning tool, not a default", "Joyagoo's storage page currently describes a paid extension option for stored items. The published amount and duration are time-sensitive and should be confirmed in the account before purchase. An extension can be sensible when a high-priority item is waiting for a compatible order, when a seller delay disrupted the plan, or when a route is temporarily unsuitable. It should not replace basic tracking. Compare the extension cost with the benefit of waiting and the risk of delaying the entire group. If only one small item is near the deadline, it may be better to ship or separate that item than to postpone a larger parcel. Save the extension receipt and new date. Never assume repeated extensions are unlimited unless the current platform explicitly shows that option."],
      ["Finish QC before parcel planning", "A stored item should have a documented keep, return or unresolved decision before it is assigned to a parcel. Joyagoo's guidance places QC before storage and parcel submission, and its published return window is much shorter than the free-storage period. Storage time therefore cannot be used as extra time for an after-sales decision. Review identity, variation, visible condition and measurements promptly when QC completes. If an eligible return is needed, use the live action while it remains available. Once the item is accepted, note packaging requirements and any fragile or restricted features. This prevents the parcel stage from reopening old product questions when the practical return option has already disappeared. A clean parcel queue contains only accepted items with clear packing instructions."],
      ["Leave time for packing and payment", "Do not wait until the final storage day to submit a complex parcel. The warehouse may need to pack, weigh or process a rehearsal request, and you may need to compare updated routes or pay a difference. Set an internal action date at least several working days before the earliest account deadline, with more margin for a large or mixed parcel. Confirm the destination address, selected items, packaging services, insurance choice and route eligibility before submitting. The shopping guidance explains that an estimated fee can differ from the final packed fee. Keep enough account and budget flexibility for that process. Once submitted, monitor parcel status and pay within the current parcel-storage window. A visible buffer is cheaper than making a rushed decision with incomplete route information."],
      ["Keep evidence until the parcel leaves", "Maintain the item list, stored dates, QC images, extension records, parcel selection, packing instructions and payment receipt in one folder. Remove private address and payment details before sharing screenshots. After dispatch, add final weight, dimensions, selected line and tracking number to the private record. This produces a complete timeline from Stored to parcel handoff and makes support questions specific. It also gives you evidence for future planning: which seller arrivals caused the wait, which boxes increased volume, and how much buffer was actually used. The official help center and live account remain the source of current deadlines. Your tracker is valuable because it turns those changing dates into timely actions without replacing the platform's rules."],
      ["Use a weekly storage review with explicit actions", "A useful review takes ten minutes and ends with an action for every open row. Confirm the current status in the account, update the earliest deadline, mark QC as accepted or unresolved, and assign the item to a draft parcel group. For items still waiting on the seller, decide whether their uncertain arrival is worth delaying older stored goods. For an item approaching the internal planning date, choose among submitting it, grouping it with ready products, checking the current extension option or removing a blocked item from the parcel. Review address readiness and route restrictions before the situation becomes urgent. Keep a separate flag for a submitted parcel awaiting payment because that stage follows a different clock. If support is needed, send the order or parcel number with the exact date and requested action. The purpose of the review is not to watch a countdown. It is to convert storage time into deliberate consolidation, packing and payment decisions while there is still enough margin to respond to new information."],
    ],
    sources: [
      { title: "Joyagoo Warehouse Storage Period", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/how-long-can-the-goods-be-stored-in-the-joyagoo-warehouse/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Guidance", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/shopping-assistant-guidance/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Order Statuses", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/status-of-the-shopping-agent-orders/" },
    ] satisfies Source[],
  },
  "joyagoo-returns-refunds-guide": {
    title: "Joyagoo Returns and Refunds: QC Rejection, Deadlines and Evidence",
    description: "Prepare a Joyagoo return or refund request with timely QC review, eligibility checks, precise evidence, packaging records and realistic refund tracking.",
    updated: "September 21, 2026",
    read: "12 min read",
    sections: [
      ["Treat QC completion as an action point", "Joyagoo's current return policy states that eligible requests should be made within five days, or 120 hours, after the order status changes to QC Completed. The official shopping guidance likewise tells buyers to review photos promptly. That window is not extra warehouse storage time and should not be measured from the day you happen to open the page. Save the status timestamp shown in the account, inspect the images immediately and confirm whether the return or exchange action is still present. If the button has disappeared, do not assume an article can restore it. Contact support with the order number and current status. Build a reminder at QC completion rather than waiting until several items are ready to ship. A quick, structured review preserves more options than a detailed review started after the published period."],
      ["Check eligibility before arguing about quality", "The published return page distinguishes items that qualify from categories and situations that may not. Custom-made goods, some opened or hygiene-sensitive products, removed tags or accessories, second-hand goods and other seller-defined cases can be restricted. Seller agreement, the third-party platform's rules and resale condition also matter. Open the live order and current policy, then check the exact listing terms. Do not describe the five-day statement as a universal no-questions-asked guarantee. A visible return action is useful evidence, but the final process can still require packaging, balance and seller cooperation. If eligibility is unclear, ask a narrow question with the product type and order status. This keeps policy uncertainty separate from the factual question of what was ordered and what arrived."],
      ["Create evidence that an agent can use", "A strong request identifies one issue at a time and links it to the order. Save the product title, selected color, size, version and quantity from the order record. Note the QC image number and describe the visible mismatch: “ordered black, received navy,” “size tag shows M, order shows L,” “zip is detached at the base,” or “one accessory is missing.” Use annotated crops only as a supplement and preserve the original photo. Avoid claims that the image cannot prove, such as long-term durability or authenticity. If the decisive area is not visible, request a targeted image or clarification through the current tools where available. Dated, specific evidence helps the agent communicate with the seller and reduces back-and-forth during a short window."],
      ["Preserve packaging and resale condition", "The return policy notes that a product must meet applicable resale and packaging requirements. Until the decision is final, do not ask the warehouse to remove tags, discard a shoe box, open sealed components or alter accessories if those actions could affect eligibility. For items with special packaging, review the current instructions before requesting value-added packing work. Keep a screenshot of the listing, order option and QC set, along with the time the return was submitted. If the product has a genuine visible defect, document the outer and inner packaging as well as the item. This does not guarantee approval, but it creates a coherent record of condition. Once a seller accepts the return, follow the account instructions rather than improvising a shipment or address."],
      ["Separate return costs from the product refund", "Joyagoo's current fee and return pages explain that return shipping and service handling may apply and can be deducted before the remaining product amount is returned to the account balance. Responsibility can differ when a confirmed quality issue exists. Because fees and seller decisions are time-sensitive, ask for the current amount in the live order instead of treating an old published example as permanent. Record the product amount actually paid, domestic shipping, any return charge, deduction and final balance credit as separate lines. A refund of the product price does not necessarily erase every cost already incurred. Keeping those fields separate prevents confusion when the credited balance differs from the original checkout total."],
      ["Track each stage of the refund", "After submitting, save the request time, case or order number, stated reason and evidence. Record whether the agent is waiting for the seller, whether the seller accepted, whether the item left the warehouse and when the account balance changed. Avoid opening duplicate tickets unless instructed, because parallel conversations can fragment the record. If a status appears stalled, ask for the current stage and the next required action rather than demanding an unsupported completion date. The agent acts as a communication bridge with the third-party seller, so timing can depend on more than one party. A simple event timeline gives support a clear basis for checking the case and helps you identify whether the delay concerns approval, domestic return transit or balance credit."],
      ["Handle an exchange as a new option decision", "An exchange should not be treated as a silent correction. Confirm the replacement color, size, version and quantity in writing, check whether price or domestic shipping changes, and ask how the new seller dispatch will appear in the order. When the replacement reaches the warehouse, inspect its own QC evidence rather than assuming the earlier issue is resolved. Record the new size label and measurements if fit caused the exchange. The same published after-sales rules may not restart in exactly the way a buyer expects, so use the live status and available actions as the source of truth. If the item is needed for a deadline, compare the uncertainty of an exchange with a return and separate reorder. Do not promise a faster result based on another buyer's case."],
      ["Use a calm decision rule", "Keep the item when it matches the order, the visible condition is acceptable and the remaining uncertainty is something QC cannot resolve. Request clarification when one decisive fact is missing and there is still time to act. Request a return or exchange when the order record and warehouse evidence show a material mismatch or an eligible issue you are unwilling to accept. Escalate with a dated timeline when the live process stops moving. This rule focuses attention on evidence rather than perfection. It also connects returns with the broader workflow: verify the listing before payment, preserve the exact option, review QC quickly, protect resale condition and keep deductions visible. Current official policy and account controls govern the actual outcome; this guide organizes the work needed to use them effectively."],
      ["Close the case with a reconciliation record", "When the return, exchange or refund finishes, record the result instead of deleting the working files. Note the item amount, accepted reason, seller response, domestic return movement, deductions, credited balance and completion date. If the outcome differs from the request, preserve the explanation provided by the agent. For an exchange, link the replacement order and its new QC decision. For a refund, compare the credit with the product amount actually paid and keep payment-channel effects separate. This reconciliation helps identify whether the remaining difference is domestic shipping, handling, currency treatment or another documented charge. It also improves the next order: an unclear option may suggest a better pre-purchase note, while a hidden area may suggest a more targeted QC request. Keep private identifiers secure and share only redacted evidence. A closed record is valuable because it turns one after-sales event into a repeatable prevention checklist without pretending that another product or seller will produce the same outcome."],
    ],
    sources: [
      { title: "Joyagoo Return Policy", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/terms-of-promised-returns-with-no-reasons/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Guidance", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/shopping-assistant-guidance/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Agent Fee Explanation", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/how-does-shopping-agent-service-charge/" },
    ] satisfies Source[],
  },
  "joyagoo-fees-explained": {
    title: "Joyagoo Fees Explained 2026: Product, Payment and Shipping Costs",
    description: "Map Joyagoo costs by stage so product price, domestic freight, optional services, returns, international shipping and customs are not mixed together.",
    updated: "September 21, 2026",
    read: "12 min read",
    sections: [
      ["Use a staged cost model", "The useful way to understand Joyagoo fees is to follow when each cost appears. At the product stage, the buyer pays the item price and Chinese domestic shipping shown in the order flow. During warehouse handling, optional services or an after-sales return may create additional charges. At parcel submission, international shipping depends on destination, route, packed weight, volume and product restrictions. Customs or taxes can arise separately under the destination and line. Mixing these stages produces misleading “price per item” claims. Create columns for product, domestic freight, payment or conversion, warehouse add-ons, return costs, international freight and destination charges. Enter zero only when the live flow actually confirms zero; otherwise mark the amount unknown. This worksheet exposes which part of the total is fixed, estimated or still waiting for a later decision."],
      ["Separate seller money from service decisions", "Joyagoo's published fee explanation says the payment for goods and Chinese domestic shipping is transferred to the seller through the platform, and describes the shopping agent service itself as free. That statement does not mean the complete journey has no costs. The same page lists possible return handling, international parcel shipping and customs charges, while the shopping guidance describes optional value-added services. Read “free agent service” narrowly and preserve the rest of the cost map. A seller can also change the live item price or domestic shipping, so a spreadsheet snapshot is not a quote. Record the amount shown for the selected variation at checkout and save the date. This keeps a promotional headline from obscuring later operational costs."],
      ["Check payment and currency at the moment of use", "A displayed USD, EUR or GBP reference may not match the exact amount charged by a payment method. Currency conversion, card issuer treatment and payment-channel charges can change over time and by account. Review the live payment page before confirming and compare the platform amount with the final issuer amount after settlement. Record the source currency, displayed rate if provided, payment-channel fee if shown and final card or wallet debit. Do not calculate a permanent markup from one transaction. Refunds can also return to an account balance rather than reversing every external payment effect. Keeping payment evidence separate from item and shipping costs makes the final comparison honest without making unsupported claims about a provider's current rate."],
      ["Treat optional services as itemized choices", "Extra detailed photos, measurements, packaging removal, reinforcement, insurance and rehearsal packing should be recorded only when selected and currently offered. Each option should solve a specific problem. A measurement can reduce fit uncertainty; reinforcement can protect a fragile parcel; removing a box can reduce volume; rehearsal can improve pre-dispatch weight evidence. None is automatically valuable for every order. Open the live service list, note the current charge and state what decision it supports. Avoid copying an old help-center number into a permanent budget because service prices can change. The correct question is not “which add-ons are cheap?” but “which uncertainty or risk is this add-on reducing, and is the evidence worth the current cost and added weight?”"],
      ["Estimate shipping with both weight scenarios", "International parcel cost cannot be inferred from product weight alone. The packed parcel includes retained retail packaging, protective material and the outer carton. Some routes compare actual weight with volumetric or dimensional weight, calculated from length, width, height and a route-specific divisor. Build two scenarios using the current route rules: one with expected actual weight and another with estimated packed dimensions. Compare eligibility, tracking, insurance terms, restrictions and size limits along with price. The shopping guidance says the initial international fee can be an estimate and the final fee is calculated after packing from the actual size and weight. Leave a budget buffer and record the final measurements. A route screenshot from another country or month is not a reliable quote for the current parcel."],
      ["Budget returns as a separate branch", "If an item is returned after warehouse receipt, current official pages say shipping and service handling may apply and the remaining refund can be credited to the account balance. The exact responsibility can depend on whether a quality issue is confirmed and on seller rules. In the worksheet, branch the item into “keep” and “return” scenarios. The keep branch continues to international shipping. The return branch includes any domestic return and handling deduction, then records the credited amount and date. Do not subtract the original product price twice or assume domestic freight is always recoverable. This branch makes the financial consequence visible before an emotional keep-or-return decision and helps reconcile the balance later."],
      ["Keep customs and tax language conditional", "Destination charges vary by country, item, declared information, shipping method and current law. Joyagoo's fee page states that customs charges may vary depending on the shipping method, while its shopping guidance warns that international parcels carry customs and tax risk. Do not promise “tax free,” a fixed threshold or guaranteed clearance without current official route and government evidence. Treat any estimate as a planning allowance rather than a legal conclusion. Preserve accurate product descriptions and follow the live declaration instructions. For higher-value or regulated items, consult the destination authority or qualified adviser. A responsible cost guide identifies the uncertainty instead of hiding it inside a confident landed-cost number."],
      ["Close the worksheet with actuals", "After delivery, replace estimates with the values that actually occurred: item payment, domestic shipping, payment debit, selected services, return deductions, final international freight, refunds and destination charges. Keep the route, packed weight, dimensions and date next to the cost. Then compare estimate versus actual by stage. This shows whether the largest error came from a changed product option, packaging volume, route choice or a fee omitted from the first plan. Do not generalize one parcel into a universal Joyagoo cost per kilogram. Use the completed record to improve the next estimate while rechecking every live price and policy. The most accurate fee guide is a transparent worksheet whose unknowns disappear as the order moves through the real workflow."],
      ["Use a three-column budget before committing", "Divide the planned total into confirmed, estimated and contingent amounts. Confirmed values are the selected item price and domestic shipping shown at the moment of order, plus any payment amount already settled. Estimated values include services you may choose and international freight based on current weight, dimensions and route options. Contingent values cover a return, final packing adjustment, customs event or other outcome that has not occurred. Give every figure a source date and remove it when the live page changes. This format prevents the most optimistic number from being presented as the expected total. It also makes comparison fair: two products should be compared at the same stage and with the same known fields. Before paying, test whether the purchase still makes sense if the estimated amount rises within a reasonable range and a contingent cost occurs. If the answer is no, reduce the parcel, choose a better-documented item or wait for stronger evidence. Review the worksheet again at QC completion and after final packing, because those are the points where uncertainty becomes an actionable keep, return or shipping choice. A responsible budget does not predict every fee; it shows exactly where uncertainty remains and who or what will resolve it."],
    ],
    sources: [
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Agent Fee Explanation", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/how-does-shopping-agent-service-charge/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Guidance", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/shopping-assistant-guidance/" },
      { title: "Actual and Volumetric Weight", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/actual-weight-and-volumetric-weight/" },
    ] satisfies Source[],
  },
  "joyagoo-shipping-to-usa-guide": {
    title: "Joyagoo Shipping to the USA: Weight, Routes and Customs Checklist",
    description: "Prepare a Joyagoo parcel for the USA with address, restriction, chargeable-weight, packing, route, customs and tracking checks based on live options.",
    updated: "September 21, 2026",
    read: "12 min read",
    sections: [
      ["Confirm the destination record before packing", "A Joyagoo shipping plan to the USA begins with an accurate recipient record, not with a route price. Enter the recipient name, street number and name, apartment or unit, city, state abbreviation, ZIP code and phone details exactly as the live form requests. Compare the finished record with a reliable address source and remove accidental non-Latin characters introduced by copy and paste. Do not publish or share the completed address in screenshots. If a forwarding address, campus, military format or remote territory is involved, confirm that the current line accepts it before packing. A route can be inexpensive and still be unusable for the address. Save a private redacted copy showing only the field structure so support can diagnose formatting without exposing personal data."],
      ["Classify every item for route eligibility", "Make a parcel inventory with product name, material, battery or liquid status, magnetic component, brand or intellectual-property sensitivity, food or cosmetic classification and any other restriction shown in the account. Joyagoo's shopping guidance says available lines can be screened according to the products in the parcel. Do not hide an item to preserve a preferred route. If one product removes most options, compare a separate parcel rather than forcing the complete group onto an unsuitable line. Recheck restrictions on the submission date because carrier acceptance can change. A spreadsheet category such as accessories or electronics is too broad for this task; the live item description and current platform classification are what matter."],
      ["Calculate chargeable weight, not product weight", "Record the stored weight of each item, retained packaging and expected protective material. Then estimate the final outer dimensions. For any line using a volumetric rule, calculate length × width × height divided by the current divisor shown for that service and compare the result with actual weight. The billed value may be the greater amount, subject to thresholds and rounding rules. A light pair of boxed shoes can therefore cost more than its scale weight suggests, while dense clothing may remain actual-weight driven. Do not reuse a divisor from another route. The official help center explains that weighing standards differ among transportation services. Keep both calculations beside the dated route screenshot so the assumption remains traceable."],
      ["Choose packaging by damage risk and dimensions", "Removing seller packaging can reduce weight or volume, but it may expose structured shoes, bags, electronics or fragile hardware. Decide item by item. Compress soft clothing only when construction will tolerate it. Retain protection around sharp edges, screens, glass and crush-sensitive forms. Reinforcement can reduce damage risk but also increase chargeable weight, so state the reason for every request. If the parcel has several heavy and soft items, consider internal separation or a split. A rehearsal service can improve the evidence available before final dispatch, but it does not eliminate logistics or customs risk. Save the requested instructions and compare them with the packed result before payment."],
      ["Compare live routes across six fields", "Build a table for every currently available USA line: product eligibility, estimated and final billing method, stated delivery range, tracking coverage, insurance or compensation terms, and maximum dimensions or weight. Add the displayed price only after those fields. No single route is universally best. A lower price can come with a different handoff, tracking pattern, protection level or restriction. Published delivery ranges are estimates rather than guarantees because customs, carrier transfers, weather and local delivery can intervene. Use the live options for the exact parcel and destination instead of a community list from another date. Save the selection rationale so a later tracking delay can be evaluated against the route that was actually purchased."],
      ["Prepare customs information without guessing", "Use accurate, consistent item descriptions, quantities and values in the live declaration flow. Do not label a commercial product as a gift merely to pursue a different outcome, and do not rely on an old social-media threshold as legal advice. U.S. customs treatment and carrier processes can change, and the applicable result depends on the shipment details. Joyagoo's official guidance warns that taxes, inspection, seizure and delays are risks outside the agent's full control. Review current platform instructions and, when the value or product category warrants it, current U.S. government guidance. Keep purchase and declaration records. A truthful, readable declaration is more defensible than a vague description designed around an unsupported promise."],
      ["Track handoffs with a dated timeline", "After dispatch, save the parcel number, route, tracking page, dispatch date, packed weight and dimensions. International tracking may pause between export, transport, customs and the domestic carrier. Record each scan with its time and location instead of treating every quiet period as loss. If the stated route range is exceeded or an exception appears, contact support with the parcel number and the last meaningful event. Avoid opening multiple cases with different descriptions. When the domestic carrier receives the parcel, use its own tracking record for local delivery details. Photograph visible outer damage before opening and keep the packaging until contents are checked. This timeline supports a specific claim if something goes wrong."],
      ["Use a final USA pre-submit checklist", "Before payment, confirm address, item inventory, route eligibility, actual and volumetric scenarios, packaging instructions, insurance choice, declaration, coupon if valid and the budget buffer for a packing adjustment. Verify that every item has passed QC and that no open return decision is being buried in the parcel. Compare the final packed figures with the estimate and resolve unexplained changes. Store personal documents privately and share only redacted evidence. This process cannot guarantee delivery time or customs treatment, but it removes preventable errors and makes the remaining risk visible. The best USA shipping choice is the live route that fits the actual product mix, address, dimensions and protection needs—not a line selected from an undated recommendation."],
      ["Audit the final parcel against the saved plan", "After the warehouse finishes packing, compare the final item count, weight, dimensions, services and amount with the pre-submit worksheet. Investigate a major change before dispatch: a retained shoe box may explain new volume, reinforcement may explain added weight, or a missing item may indicate that the wrong warehouse selection was submitted. Confirm that the chosen USA line still appears for the final parcel and that the address has not been truncated. Save the final declaration and insurance selection with the dated route terms. If a difference is legitimate, update the budget rather than hiding it inside the original estimate. Once the parcel leaves, the same record becomes the tracking baseline. At delivery, reconcile the contents and condition against it. Record the first U.S. carrier scan and compare the local tracking number with the international parcel record. If the address contains a unit, verify that it appears on the label preview as well as in the saved profile. Keep support communication in one timeline, with dates and the most recent carrier event, so a delayed handoff is not confused with an address exception. Retain the final label and packed-item list privately until delivery is complete. This audit creates continuity across warehouse, carrier and last-mile stages. It cannot control customs or transit, but it gives the buyer a coherent set of facts for support and prevents a last-minute interface change from passing unnoticed."],
    ],
    sources: [
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Guidance", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/shopping-assistant-guidance/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Weighing Standards", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/weighing-standards-for-each-transportation-services/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Mail Restrictions", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/mail-restrictions/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Customs and Taxes", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/customs-taxes/" },
    ] satisfies Source[],
  },
  "joyagoo-shipping-to-uk-guide": {
    title: "Joyagoo Shipping to the UK: VAT, Customs and Parcel Preparation",
    description: "Prepare Joyagoo shipping to the UK with a clean address, accurate item record, live route comparison, chargeable-weight check and cautious VAT planning.",
    updated: "September 21, 2026",
    read: "12 min read",
    sections: [
      ["Create a UK-ready address record", "Start with the name, house number and street, flat or unit, locality when needed, post town, postcode and phone fields exactly as the live form requests. UK postcodes contain letters, numbers and a space, so compare the final entry with a trusted address record. Avoid repeating the county in a way that displaces a required field, and check that autocomplete has not changed the flat or building. Keep a redacted screenshot for troubleshooting but never publish the recipient's full details. Some route descriptions can have postcode, island or remote-area limits. Confirm eligibility before packing, not after paying for an unsuitable line. A clean address record prevents a low-level field error from undermining every later route and customs decision."],
      ["Describe the product mix precisely", "List every item with a plain product description, material where relevant, quantity, battery or liquid status, and any restriction shown in the account. Do not rely on a spreadsheet label such as accessories to decide transport eligibility. Joyagoo's official shopping flow says the system can screen available lines based on the products selected. If a cosmetic, battery, magnetic component, food item or other controlled category changes the route list, compare a separate parcel or remove it from the plan until the current rules are clear. Do not misdescribe the item to force an option to appear. Route acceptance can change, so save the live result and date for the actual parcel."],
      ["Model actual and volumetric weight", "Add the warehouse weights, retained seller packaging, protection and outer carton to estimate actual weight. Estimate the packed length, width and height separately. Where the live UK line uses volumetric billing, apply its current divisor and compare the result with actual weight, including any threshold or rounding rule. A light but bulky jacket or shoe box can make dimensions decisive. Dense folded clothing may remain actual-weight driven. Removing packaging is useful only when the reduction outweighs damage risk. Keep the formula, units and route name in the record. The official weighing guidance shows that different services can apply different standards, so there is no responsible universal divisor or price per kilogram."],
      ["Compare routes beyond the tax label", "For every route currently shown, record eligibility, billing method, estimated range, tracking, insurance or compensation, handoff, maximum dimensions and the wording used for tax or customs handling. Do not translate a marketing label into a legal guarantee. A route described as tax-inclusive, duty-paid or similar should be evaluated from its current terms for the exact destination and parcel, not from an old community screenshot. The cheapest line may not fit a fragile or high-value mix, and the fastest estimate may have stricter limits. Save the route description and selection date. This makes it possible to distinguish what the platform displayed from later assumptions when a carrier or customs event occurs."],
      ["Plan VAT and customs cautiously", "UK VAT, duty and import procedures depend on shipment value, product type, seller or platform treatment, declaration and the route used. Rules and thresholds can change, so this guide does not provide a guaranteed tax outcome. Use accurate descriptions and values, follow the current declaration flow and read the current route terms. Do not mark goods as gifts or lower values merely to pursue a preferred result. For a higher-value, commercial or regulated parcel, check current HMRC guidance or obtain qualified advice before dispatch. Joyagoo's own shopping guidance identifies taxes, customs delays and seizure among international-shipping risks outside its full control. Build a contingency into the budget instead of treating an informal “tax free” statement as proof."],
      ["Match protection to the item", "Soft clothing can often be folded efficiently, but heavy, rigid or fragile items need protection that may add dimensions. Decide whether to keep shoe boxes, retail packaging and internal supports based on structure and purpose. Use moisture protection where it addresses a real exposure and reinforcement where crushing or impact is plausible. Avoid vague instructions such as “pack well”; state which surface, edge or component needs protection. A rehearsal or pre-pack service can provide better weight and dimension evidence before final dispatch if it is currently offered. Compare the packed result with the request and ask about a material discrepancy before paying. Packaging is part of the route decision because it changes both damage risk and chargeable weight."],
      ["Read tracking by logistics stage", "Save the parcel ID, line, dispatch date, final measurements and first tracking number. Expect the record to move through export handling, international transport, customs or clearance and a UK last-mile carrier. Scans may be delayed at handoffs. Build a dated timeline and use the carrier's own record once a local number appears. If the published range is exceeded or an exception code appears, send support the parcel number, route and last meaningful scan. Do not infer confiscation from one quiet period. On delivery, photograph external damage before opening and compare contents with the parcel list. Keep packaging until the item check is complete, particularly if insurance or a claim process might apply."],
      ["Complete the UK submission review", "Before final payment, verify the address and postcode, item list, restrictions, declaration, actual and volumetric estimates, route terms, protection requests, insurance choice and available budget. Confirm that every product has a finished QC decision and no return request is still open. Recheck the final packed weight and dimensions rather than assuming the first deposit remains exact. Keep private evidence securely and redact addresses, phone numbers, balances and tracking identifiers before sharing. This checklist cannot promise a delivery date or VAT result. It does ensure that the chosen route is based on the current UK destination, real contents, chargeable dimensions and visible terms instead of an undated recommendation."],
      ["Reconcile the UK parcel after final packing", "Compare the warehouse result with the worksheet before dispatch. Verify the number of items, retained boxes, protective services, actual weight, dimensions and chargeable weight under the selected line. Reopen the route description and confirm that its eligibility and customs wording have not changed during packing. Check that the postcode and flat or building information remain complete in the final address preview. Preserve the declaration, amount paid, insurance choice and dated terms in a private folder. If the packed dimensions are materially higher, identify the cause and evaluate whether repacking or a different live route is still available; do not assume that the initial deposit or tax label answers the new scenario. After dispatch, connect any local-carrier number to the original parcel and use the same timeline for export, clearance and delivery events. If a charge or document request appears, verify the sender through official carrier channels before paying or submitting personal information. Compare the request with the saved declaration, parcel value and route wording, and ask the carrier for a written explanation when the basis is unclear. After delivery, record any carrier charge or customs event separately from the international freight payment, and retain evidence until the contents have been checked. Note whether delivery went to the recipient, a neighbor, a collection point or another authorized location. Keep that delivery event dated. This final reconciliation turns an estimate into an auditable result and improves later planning without converting one UK parcel into a universal VAT, timing or route promise."],
    ],
    sources: [
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Guidance", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/shopping-assistant-guidance/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Weighing Standards", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/weighing-standards-for-each-transportation-services/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Customs and Taxes", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/customs-taxes/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Logistics Platform Overview", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/logistics-platform-introduction/" },
    ] satisfies Source[],
  },
  "joyagoo-app-guide": {
    title: "Joyagoo App Guide 2026: Product Links, QC Photos and Parcel Workflow",
    description: "Use the Joyagoo app or mobile site as a controlled workflow for live product options, order statuses, QC evidence, storage dates and parcel preparation.",
    updated: "September 21, 2026",
    read: "12 min read",
    sections: [
      ["Verify the official mobile entry point", "Begin from Joyagoo's current official website or its own published download path. App-store search results, advertising pages and similarly named services can change, so confirm the publisher, destination domain and account flow before entering credentials. Do not install a package from a community file link merely because it uses the correct logo. If no official app is available for your device or region, use the current mobile website rather than forcing an unofficial download. Save the official entry as a bookmark and enable device-level updates where appropriate. This guide focuses on the workflow shared by the mobile account experience: finding a product, preserving the exact option, monitoring order status, reviewing QC, tracking storage and preparing a parcel. Interface labels can move, so follow the current controls rather than a screenshot from an older version."],
      ["Move a spreadsheet find into a live product check", "A Joyagoo spreadsheet is a discovery tool, not a substitute for the destination listing. Open the saved route on mobile and confirm that it resolves to the expected product. Match the title, main image, product category and option structure. Expand seller details that are collapsed on the small screen. A base price may belong to another variation, so tap the desired color, size, version and quantity and note any price change before adding the item. If the route is dead or opens a different product, return to the spreadsheet and choose another candidate. Keep a private note with the live URL and check date. This makes mobile browsing deliberate instead of allowing a familiar thumbnail to carry an outdated assumption."],
      ["Capture the option without losing context", "Mobile screens make it easy to screenshot only the selected color while omitting the product title or size. Create one working record that includes the product, selected option text, quantity, current item amount and domestic shipping shown before payment. Preserve the seller's wording for similar variations. If a translation is ambiguous, keep the original term beside your note and ask for clarification rather than inventing a meaning. Do not place passwords, full payment details or private addresses in the screenshot folder. The goal is a clean comparison source for the later order record and warehouse photos. Before confirming payment, reopen the cart line and check every field. A correct link with the wrong option is still a wrong order."],
      ["Read mobile order statuses as events", "Joyagoo's official guidance describes a sequence including Order Processing, Purchased, Seller Sent, Stock Arrived, QC Completed and warehouse storage. Each status tells you which evidence should exist and who can act next. Purchased does not mean the seller has dispatched. Seller Sent concerns domestic movement toward the warehouse. Stock Arrived precedes inspection, and QC Completed is the point for prompt photo review. Turn on only useful notifications and verify important changes inside the account rather than relying on a notification preview. Record unexpected delays with the order number and last status. Contact the agent from the relevant order when possible so the product context remains attached. Do not create a duplicate order merely because a seller shipment is slower than another buyer's example."],
      ["Review QC photos on a larger canvas when needed", "A phone is convenient for alerts, but a small screen can hide stitching, labels, damage and measurement endpoints. First confirm product type, color, size tag, version and quantity. Zoom into front, back, sides and category-specific details without judging a compressed thumbnail. Rotate the screen or use a desktop for a final decision when symmetry or measurements matter. If an essential area is missing, request one targeted image through the current tools if available. The published return process currently uses a five-day, 120-hour period after QC Completed for eligible items, so do not postpone review until every order arrives. QC supports visible-condition and order-accuracy decisions; it does not guarantee fit, authenticity or durability."],
      ["Track storage with calendar reminders", "The official warehouse page currently states that an item receives 90 days of free storage from the Stored status. Save that date for each item and create an earlier planning reminder. Do not calculate the whole parcel from the latest arrival. The oldest stored item sets the practical schedule while newer items and seller deliveries may still be moving. Separate item storage from a submitted parcel waiting for shipping payment; the official page describes a different free period for that stage. Use a private table or calendar, not public screenshots of the account. Review the earliest date weekly and decide whether to consolidate, extend where currently available, separate a late item or submit the parcel with a safe buffer."],
      ["Prepare the parcel from current mobile options", "Select only items with completed QC decisions. Confirm the delivery address, product restrictions, packaging requests and route list shown for the exact destination and contents. Compare actual and volumetric weight rules, tracking, insurance terms, delivery estimate, size limits and compensation—not only the first price. Joyagoo's guidance says the estimated international fee can change after final packing and logistics measurement. If a complex comparison is difficult on mobile, save the candidate routes and finish on a larger screen. Recheck the final packed weight, dimensions and amount before payment. Mobile convenience should not compress a multi-field shipping decision into one tap."],
      ["Keep the account secure and the record useful", "Use a unique password, device lock and only the current official login path. Avoid saving payment credentials in shared browsers and log out of public devices. Redact name, address, phone, balance, parcel number and tracking number before asking a community for general help. Keep the private originals: order confirmation, selected variation, QC images, return request, storage dates, parcel choice, final measurements and payment receipt. Those records make support communication specific and let you compare estimates with results after delivery. The best mobile workflow is not the one with the most screenshots. It is the one that preserves the product context, prompts timely action and keeps sensitive information out of public view."],
      ["Use mobile for alerts and the best screen for the decision", "Assign each device a sensible role. Use the phone to receive status changes, open the relevant order quickly and capture a time-sensitive action. Use a larger screen when the decision depends on several QC images, tiny labels, measurement endpoints, route tables or long terms. Keep one synchronized private note with the order number, last status, next action and deadline so a device switch does not lose context. Before submitting any return or parcel, reopen the live account and verify that the saved screenshot still matches the current state. Avoid making a high-impact choice from a notification banner or compressed preview. When an update changes the interface, locate the order by its record rather than assuming a familiar button performs the same action. Review app permissions and notification settings periodically, remove downloads from unofficial sources, and keep recovery information current. Back up the minimum evidence needed for open cases. This division of work preserves the speed that makes an app useful while reducing errors caused by limited screen space. It also makes mobile use safer: the smallest necessary information stays in alerts, sensitive records remain private, and the decisive comparison happens where every field can be read clearly."],
    ],
    sources: [
      { title: "Joyagoo Shopping Guidance", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/shopping-assistant-guidance/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Order Statuses", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/status-of-the-shopping-agent-orders/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Warehouse Storage Period", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/how-long-can-the-goods-be-stored-in-the-joyagoo-warehouse/" },
      { title: "Joyagoo Return Policy", href: "https://mgt.joyagoo.com/help-center/terms-of-promised-returns-with-no-reasons/" },
    ] satisfies Source[],
  },
} as const;

